# {{project.name}}

```bash
mcpp run                        # under the emulator
mcpp run --features hardware    # on a board, over a debug probe
mcpp run --list-runners         # what else this project can do
```

The first command needs nothing installed: the board package declares the
emulator it needs and mcpp provisions it on the first `mcpp run`. `mcpp build`
does not install it — the emulator is on the `run` tier, so compiling firmware
in CI downloads nothing.

## When the board arrives

Add `--features hardware`. That is the whole change: the source, the manifest
and the command are the same. `probe-rs` is installed the first time you ask,
and only then.

| | |
|---|---|
| `mcpp run` | flash, reset, stream output, report the exit status |
| `mcpp run --runner flash` | write the image and stop — what a production line does |
| `mcpp run --runner serve` | a debug server for an IDE to attach to |

`mcpp run --list-runners` prints what this project actually supplies, which is
not a list the engine holds: a package names its own runners, and mcpp knows
none of them.

## Changing the part

`[build] target` in `mcpp.toml`. mcpp carries seven M-profile rows and they are
not interchangeable — an object built for `thumbv7em` uses instructions a
Cortex-M0 does not have. The memory map lives in the board package's linker
script; a real part needs its own, and a project supplies one by writing
`mcpp::link_script(...)` in its own `build.mcpp`.
